## OCC Auto-loan pricing and risk research

This is my strongest direct Auto pricing evidence.

I supported a major longitudinal study of long-term Auto loans and was publicly acknowledged by the authors for **“excellent research support.”**

The study used:

- 875,516 unique Auto loans;
- 2,363,261 loan-year observations;
- longitudinal credit-bureau attribute and tradeline data;
- loan terms from two years or less through seven-plus years;
- loan-age and term interactions;
- prime and subprime segmentation;
- 90-days-past-due as the core delinquency event;
- prepayment and loan maturity as competing exits;
- borrower and account characteristics;
- county unemployment;
- local house-price change;
- Manheim used-vehicle-price change;
- lender type and origination-year effects.

Important pricing work included:

- deriving APR from origination amount, monthly payment, and loan term;
- validating APR stability over time;
- comparing aggregate APR results with Experian AutoCount;
- defining spread as APR minus a same-maturity Treasury rate from the origination month;
- constructing missing four-year and six-year Treasury benchmarks from adjacent maturities;
- comparing spreads across term, origination year, credit-score segment, and lender type;
- using OLS pricing analysis;
- evaluating whether higher residual delinquency risk was reflected in price.

The research found, after controlling for observed risk factors and macroeconomic conditions, that six- and seven-plus-year Auto loans generally carried higher residual 90-DPD risk than shorter loans throughout their lives. At the same time, spreads on longer-term loans compressed relative to five-year and shorter terms, supporting evidence of an increasingly inverted Auto-loan yield curve and suggesting that incremental term risk was not fully reflected in customer pricing.

Be precise:

- The published paper is not my authorship.
- I was acknowledged for research support.
- This particular published paper used a survival-panel logistic delinquency framework and OLS pricing analysis.
- My broader OCC work also included Cox proportional hazards, discrete-time survival/logistic, binary and multinomial logistic regression, and other credit-risk methods.
- Do not incorrectly call every Auto-paper model CoxPH.

